### Verification trail (eight independent checks, all run 2026-10-24)

1. **Artificial Analysis:** `https://artificialanalysis.ai/models/omi-alpha` returns **404** — no model page.
2. **DuckDuckGo** for `"omi-alpha" model`: an exact-phrase open-web search returns **"No results found"** — nothing indexed anywhere under this name.
3. **Hugging Face model search** (`https://huggingface.co/models?search=omi-alpha`): returns **"No results found"** — zero models, zero orgs.
4. **OpenRouter full API catalog** (`https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/models`): the complete inventory was enumerated — **467 models, `total_count: 467`, `next: null`** (so this is exhaustive, not a page-one sample). Of the 30 substring matches for "omi", **every one is a Xiaomi MiMo slug or the word "omnimodal"**. There is no standalone `omi` or `omi-alpha` model ID anywhere in OpenRouter's entire catalog.
5. **models.dev canonical table:** searched for any lab or model named "Omi" — **no match**. The only substring hits are Xiaomi MiMo entries. (For contrast, `qwen3.8-omni-flash` exists but is "omni", unrelated.)
6. **OpenCode Zen docs** (`https://opencode.ai/docs/zen`): the full endpoint table, the full per-1M pricing table, and the stealth/free-models sections were all read. **No "Omi" or "omi-alpha" anywhere.** The only explicitly stealth model on Zen is **Big Pickle**; the free models are GLM-5.3-Flash Free, DeepSeek V4.1 Flash Free, Beam Free, Flint 40B Free, MiMo-V2.6-Flash Free, the three Nemotron Free tiers, and Jev 1.13 Free.
7. **Bing** for `"omi-alpha" LLM OR model OR stealth`: returned only irrelevant Wikipedia homepage spam; **zero relevant hits** for the exact string.
8. **OpenCode usage-data catalog** (`https://opencode.ai/data/`): the page and its tables (top-18 at 7d and 1d, daily tables, retention, labs) were checked. **No omi-alpha** appears. The sole unknown-lab entry currently listed is **`iceman`** (617B tokens/7d, 183B/day, status "New"). Notably, **`omen-alpha` itself no longer appears** in the current top tables — it was withdrawn after its limited window and only partially restored, which is consistent with the stealth-route lifecycle documented in `model/omen-alpha/`.

### Conflict / resolution notes

- No conflicting sources were found — all eight agree independently that nothing exists under this name.
- **The exhaustive OpenRouter check is the strongest single piece of evidence**: a full 467-model inventory with a null `next` cursor rules out pagination as an explanation, so the absence is definitive rather than a sampling artifact.
- **Most likely origin — a spelling corruption of a real sibling.** `omen-alpha` is a verified-real stealth model (OpenCode Go, released 2026-09-04, 500K context / 128K output, text+image in, reasoning, vendor unconfirmed — see `model/omen-alpha/`). The two slugs differ by one character and sit adjacent in this roster, which makes a one-off Levenshtein error the most plausible explanation for this folder. Nothing was merged or renamed on the strength of that inference — it is recorded as a hypothesis, not acted on, because `RULES.md` requires per-instance user sign-off for slug merges.
- Closest real model for anyone who needs a substitute: **`omen-alpha`**, already researched (and self-excluded on zero benchmarks) in this pass. Its own evidence base is thin — no benchmark-tracker entry and an unconfirmed vendor — but it is real, whereas this slug is not.
- **Surfaced for the orchestrator, not acted on:** this slug appears in `model-queue.md` with a computed Overall of 52, so other raters evidently produced reports for it. Under `RULES.md` a folder is permanent and only a *user* may park a slug (it must be removed from both `RETIRED_SLUGS` lists, `src/data/models.ts` + `scripts/lib/average.mjs`, to be rendered in `model-aliases.generated.md`). This file therefore makes no parking recommendation and no deletion — it records the negative finding and stops.
- **Batch note:** this is the twelfth phantom slug found in this queue, and the second (`neomorph`, `owo-2.5` being the others) where the *word itself* is common outside AI. It is also the first whose most probable origin is a single-character corruption of a real sibling slug, which is a distinct failure mode from the suffix-transplant phantoms (`gpt-oss-120b-lite`, `qwen-3.5-flash-lite`, `glm-4.6-flash`) and the invented-number phantoms (`qwen-3.8-325b`, `claude-opus-4.4`).

